{
 "_note": "GRADIENT STOP NORMALISATION and the paint-space projection. A gradient is authored as a `<gradient id kind>` child holding `<stop offset color opacity>` children, and the author is allowed to be sloppy in exactly the ways CSS and SVG allow, so the normalisation is where three renderers drift. THE LAW, in order: parse each offset (a number or a percentage), CLAMP it into 0..1; a missing FIRST offset is 0 and a missing LAST is 1; a run of missing interior offsets spaces EVENLY between its two known neighbours; then walk left to right forcing MONOTONIC non-decreasing offsets (a decreasing offset is raised to its predecessor \u2014 never re-sorted, because author order is the paint order). `stop-opacity` MULTIPLIES the colour's own alpha. Zero stops is one diagnostic and paints nothing; one stop is a solid colour. Two stops at the SAME offset are a hard stop and the LATER stop wins from that offset onward. Sampling interpolates componentwise in STRAIGHT (non-premultiplied) sRGB \u2014 not linear-light, not premultiplied \u2014 because that is what Canvas2D, CoreGraphics and Compose all do by default, and matching the platforms beats being theoretically prettier. A SEMANTIC TOKEN (accent, label, \u2026) survives normalisation UNRESOLVED: the theme resolves it at paint time, so the pure core must not bake it. `projection` pins the 1-D paint coordinate t for each gradient kind: linear is the clamped projection onto the axis, radial is the clamped distance ratio, angular is the turn fraction from `start`, measured with atan2 in the y-down canvas space and wrapped into [0,1).",
